220	01010000A0E61000005C8F6A2350A02BC0393436B620CD3C400000000000000000	228	Arecife - Las Palmas	ferry	Thu, 29 Feb 2024 23:05:00 GMT	\N	\N	\N	\N	0	waypoint	\N	\N
221	01010000A0E6100000F40DE6C296D92EC07661A86EFB203C400000000000000000	229	SY Thinking of Dave	ferry	Thu, 29 Feb 2024 23:07:00 GMT	Marc, Rob, Matty, Rika	Found a boat to go to the Carribean!	boat	\N	2	sleep	\N	\N
222	01010000A0E610000064E835A42E532EC0E92D71804CA73A400000000000000000	230	Las Palmas - Mindelo, Day 1	boat	Sat, 02 Mar 2024 23:00:00 GMT	Marc, Rob, Matty, Rika	We are off! The atmosphere is interesting, somehow everyone is calm and silent, not much talking. Somehow we all know we are embarking on something special, but for me it feels like any other sailing trips I’ve done.  The leaving and fueling up works smoothly, I’m glad that Mark is giving instructions calm and not nervously. Because Al told me that often in such a bit stressful situations, the true spirit of a crew is visible.  As soon as we leave the harbor, the waves get noticeably higher. The spray even sometimes comes into the cockpit. We set the sails quite quickly, second reef and the wind speeds of around 18-20 knots push us nicely southwards. Bruce the weather guy told us to first head towards south-east, to avoid the lee of the islands. Rika and Matty quickly get a bit seasick and head down to the cabin to nap. Luckily my patch is working and I feel superb! No seasickness and really enjoying the waves.  Dinner: Curry by Mark 	boat	SY Thinking of Dave	1	sleep	\N	\N
223	01010000A0E61000008F804EBD7A7931C0FBD41EC4F8A439400000000000000000	231	Las Palmas - Mindelo, Day 2	boat	Sun, 03 Mar 2024 23:00:00 GMT	Marc, Rob, Matty, Rika	In the evening Mark cooks a really nice curry, which we eat with the left over rice. The waves are still high, we would estimate around 3-4m. But it’s impressive to observe them.  The night was a bit rough, I kept waking up every 45min because of the rolling of the boat. Rika was quite sick and had to throw up several times. So we divided the night shifts between three, Mark joined all of us for some time. Mine is 3:00-7:00. At 4:00 we jibe and set towards a westerly course. The wind calms down a bit and everything goes smooth.  I have a bit a heavy breathing, not sure if it’s asthma or anxiety. But it’s not strong and when I talk with someone it goes away.  At the end of my shift I get tired and wake up Matty at 7, eat a bread with jam and go to sleep for another 4h. This time I sleep better and wake up fresh.  Rika and Matty feel better and are out in the cockpit. I take my first shower on the boat, it feels awesome! I still feel so comfortable and all the worries I had the last few days are gone. I’m quite happy living on this small floating thingy for the next three weeks. But I also have trouble grasping or understanding the length of this trip.  Dinner: Pasta Bolognese by Dan	boat	SY Thinking of Dave	1	sleep	\N	\N
224	01010000A0E61000009757B55A31ED32C09C15B43D080438400000000000000000	232	Las Palmas - Mindelo, Day 3	boat	Mon, 04 Mar 2024 23:00:00 GMT	Marc, Rob, Matty, Rika	I was very happy cooking dinner and managed without any seasickness. But when I tried to defrost the meat in the microwave, the whole power supply collapsed and the autopilot stopped working. Rob was shouting from the helm for help because he couldn’t see where the wind was coming from. I tried to give him directions and we managed to get the boat on course again, but not after a few circles and unwanted jibes. In the meantime Mark put the power back on and we could calm down again.  After the dinner Mark insisted on doing the washing up and wouldn’t let anyone help him. We had a beautiful sunset and then I already went to bed quickly. I tried to watch some Netflix (Rita) but got a bit dizzy and decided to sleep.  When muy shift started around 4am, the wind has shifted and came from the back. Since we already went too much north instead of south, Mark and me did a Chinese jibe together, which means to turn the boat the other way about 290° and therefore actually doing a tack.  All of a sudden an alarm signal came on, but only around ever minute. Mark investigated and found out that it’s the bilge pump isn’t working. This is quite a crucial pump, which removes water from the boat in case of a leak. Since it wasn’t working since quite some time probably, some water has collected. We alternated in manually pumping it out, with the emergency pump.  After 7 I slept again around 4 hours and woke up fresh and happy around lunchtime. Mark and Rob started to work on replacing the bilge pump, which luckily Mark had a spare of. It was very successful and after around 2h everything was working again. Also the waves and wind have calmed down and it was smooth sailing. Just when I went to the bow for the first time, the dolphins came and stayed for quite some time. I enjoyed watching them sooooo much.  Dinner: Fajitas by Mark 	boat	SY Thinking of Dave	1	sleep	\N	\N
225	01010000A0E6100000246D27C63E5D33C0A64532DF9F0836400000000000000000	233	Las Palmas - Mindelo, Day 4	boat	Tue, 05 Mar 2024 23:00:00 GMT	Marc, Rob, Matty, Rika	Well what a plot twist. Yesterday just before dinner, the plotter started to restart every other minute. Mark immediately started to investigate, but it got worse and worse. Ever minute all the power of the boat shut down, came on again for a moment and just going off again. Around 7:00 all the power was dead. Mark was dumbfounded and couldn’t figure out at all what could be the issue. We started to realize what this means:  no autopilot  no lights  no navigation system  no emergency satellite communication (SkyMate)  no VHF (apart from the handheld)  no charging for the phones/laptops  no fridge  no freezer  no waterpump (luckily there is a manual one in the kitchen)  no gas, because the switch is managed electrically  no engine to motor  No generator  No showers  No hot water  No windlass : no anchoring  But we also realized that we still can sail and use the compass and the wind pointer for navigation. Also Marks iPad had a backup plotter which we can use. Also there is a backup burner for the kitchen.  So we can advance and cook our food, which are the most important things. We decided to change the course towards Mindelo on Capo Verde, which was still 560nm away, 4-5 days sailing. Our main worry was that the family and friends were expecting daily updates on our location through SkyMate. If they don’t hear from us for the next 4 days, they will be worried to death.  Nevertheless we went into action mode and decided on 2h shifts with two people, which means you would only get around 2h if sleep between each shift. None of us could really sleep because of the fear and adrenaline. Additionally the navigation was quite difficult without the system and we made around 10 accidental jibes, which means that the boom is thrown to the other side with a loud swoooosh and boom. This always woke everyone up and could seriously damage the rigging if it continues. We also sometimes moved in circles, confused about what our course is. This night no one slept well, also the waves and the wind were pretty strong.  In the morning the waves were still high and strong, but eased down a bit after some time. We finally took down the main sail which took some damage over the night. I cooked some porridge with the spare camping stove Mark has on board. Then we tried to start fixing the electricity issue. We decided that SkyMate is the most important component on board, so my idea was to find its power input and connect it directly to the batteries, which still had power. It was quite the task to crawl around on the bottom of the boat trying to follow wire and see where it’s connected to. Finally we found the right one and I could directly put into action the things I learned from Al. I cut the wire and attached it to some clamps, which I could directly put to the batteries. It took some time then to find the spot with 12V difference, because the batteries are split into 4 units. But finally we found one, attached the clamps and screamed in joy when the little lights of the unit lit up! Immediately we checked the connection and had already three new messages sitting in our inbox. What a success! We informed the important people that everything’s alright and that we’re heading now to Mindelo.  In the afternoon it eased and everybody’s spirit got higher again. We put on music and Rika and I had a little crochet session. Then the dolphins came and stayed for several hours which was amazing. They were jumping around all sides of the boat and spread really good mood. We started to joke and say that we’re having the best time.  Dinner: Fried Rice with chicken by Mark 	boat	SY Thinking of Dave	1	sleep	\N	\N
226	01010000A0E61000000FFEC2A745F934C0227E103BACD234400000000000000000	234	Las Palmas - Mindelo, Day 5	boat	Wed, 06 Mar 2024 23:00:00 GMT	Marc, Rob, Matty, Rika	But of course this didn’t last long. At night around 11 we got an update from the weather guy Bruce which predicted unpleasant conditions up ahead. We will directly cross the trade wind surge, which he originally wanted us to go around. The wind will be around 35 knots and waves to up to 3.5-4m high. This is still not a storm and manageable with the boat but will just make the next days much harder. And indeed when I went to sleep, the rocking and throwing around made it impossible to sleep. In addition to that my head started to go in overdrive and envision all the bad things that could happen now. Every creaking sound or splash made me worried. I was scared and started to feel quite some anxiety. The resulting sleep deprivation made it even worse. I think that night I only slept around 3h. But at least whenever I was up in the cockpit I felt better, seeing the sea and being able to steer made me feel better and in control. During the day I tried to sleep more but still couldn’t. It was wrecking. I just wanted to arrive to Mindelo.  Dinner: Meatballs with thawed veggies in a wrap by Rika.	boat	SY Thinking of Dave	1	sleep	\N	\N
227	01010000A0E61000002111E490A37F36C0A7B08FDE858D33400000000000000000	235	Las Palmas - Mindelo, Day 6	boat	Thu, 07 Mar 2024 23:00:00 GMT	Marc, Rob, Matty, Rika	Yesterday evening when I couldn’t nap I felt terrible, but after some food I was ready to do the first night shift together with Rika. We listened to a True Crime podcast together which made the shift go by very quickly and quite pleasant. Then I tried to sleep but still couldn’t. So Mark offered me to take my next shift and I took a sleeping pill which knocked me out and I finally slept for 6 hours. Then I replaced Rob and did a 6 hour shift into the morning. When sitting down at the wheel, I noticed a flying fish on deck! They are very interesting creatures. The sun came up and I felt much better and went down to make scrambled eggs for everyone.  Since we didn’t have an automatic bilge pump anymore, I kept checking it regularly. I always filled up a bit and there was also some water collecting under my bed. Every time we sponged it up, it filled again gradually and it made me a bit worried. The same for the bilge, which never stayed empty and needed pumping like 2-3 times a day.  Around 2pm tried to nap a bit and actually managed to sleep two hours which made me quite happy. The seasickness patch wasn’t working anymore so I took it off. I felt some effects of the seasickness but not that strongly and could be dealt with well with the occasional pill.  Another thing we started to notice today was that the steering wheels made a strange cracking sound. It slowly started to become more and we already talked about the possibility of needing to use the emergency tiller. I hope it won’t be needed until we arrive in Mindelo.  The coast guard in Mindelo responded to our email and said that they are on standby and awaiting our arrival, which was a great relieve! They wanted regular position updates, which made me feel much safer. In case something else happens they would know where to search.  After dinner we all sat in the cockpit, watching the sunset and having pleasant conversations. I realized how glad I was that everyone was looking after each other and in general what a great team we were. And it wasn’t just born out of friendship or personal care for each other, it was more the fact that we’re all in the same boat and needed to work together. But also I felt how this situation made us get closer to everyone.  Dinner: Steak with thawed veggies in a wrap by Mark	boat	SY Thinking of Dave	1	sleep	\N	\N
228	01010000A0E6100000C31572E31C8337C083B5D4ABE10932400000000000000000	236	Las Palmas - Mindelo, Day 7	boat	Fri, 08 Mar 2024 23:00:00 GMT	Marc, Rob, Matty, Rika	The weather calmed down a bit and we settled in for another night watch. We jibed again and found a really great course that worked with the wind and will bring us more or less directly to Mindelo. Helming was rather easy and the waves pushed us nicely. I did two night shifts, one with Rob and one with Mark and we had really nice and deep conversation. However, when going to sleep after the second watch I had quite a strong anxiety attack and had trouble breathing. I took an seasickness pill, then a Temesta and also CBD oil. I then managed to sleep but just around 4-5h and the breathing issues continued. I went in the cockpit, told Rob and Mark that I’m not doing well and they were quite understanding. I played the guitar and sang which helped a lot. The day then was okay, I managed distract myself enough so that the symptoms were not too strong.  Rob and I managed to connect a USB plug to the batteries, so that we can charge our phones and headlamps. I really enjoyed the work, even though I got quite a bit seasick since my patch didn’t work anymore. We had a great dinner, and then I played a bit more guitar. I was scared of going to bed and have another anxiety attack. Right now I’m laying in bed and hope I can rest a bit. In 1.5h I have my shift together with Rika and we will listen to a True Crime podcast, which I’m already looking forward to. Worst case I just don’t sleep tonight, because tomorrow evening we will finally arrive in Mindelo, Capo Verde! I’m soooo excited and happy to finally see land and feel more safe again, I’ve been under quite some adrenaline and a pressure the last 5 days. Tomorrow will be a good day! 	boat	SY Thinking of Dave	1	sleep	\N	\N
